        <article class="doc-card">
            <header class="doc-header">
                <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
                <p class="doc-meta">Effective 26 August 2026 &middot; SACC Software Limited</p>
            </header>

            <p class="doc-lede">
                Q-Mgr is queue and visitor management software built by SACC Software Limited. Organizations
                use it to run their front desks, service counters, and visitor logs — which means it handles
                two different kinds of personal data, and this policy tries to be honest about the difference
                rather than blur it the way most privacy pages do.
            </p>

            <section class="doc-callout">
                <strong>The short version:</strong> if you're a staff member at an organization using Q-Mgr, we
                are the ones responsible for your account data, described below. If you're someone who took a
                queue ticket, checked in at a front desk, or received a message through Q-Mgr, your data was
                collected by that organization, using our platform — we just store and move it on their
                instruction. If you want it corrected or deleted, start with them, not us; see
                "If you're a customer or visitor of one of our clients" below.
            </section>

            <h2>1. Who this covers</h2>
            <p>
                Two groups of people interact with data that passes through Q-Mgr:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Our customers</strong> &mdash; the organizations (banks, hospitals, government offices,
                    telecoms, retailers, and similar) who sign up for a Q-Mgr account, and the staff they invite
                    (admins, branch managers, counter agents). We are directly responsible for this data.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Their customers and visitors</strong> &mdash; the people who take a queue ticket, are
                    checked in at a front desk, or receive an SMS/email/WhatsApp/Telegram message an organization
                    sends through our Campaign Marketing tool. Legally, the organization is the data controller
                    here and we are the data processor: they decide what to collect and why, we provide the
                    software that stores and moves it.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2>2. What we collect, and why</h2>
            <p><strong>From organizations and their staff:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Account details — name, work email, phone number, role, and a password (stored as a salted hash, never in plain text).</li>
                <li>Organization details — business name, industry, branch locations, timezone, and billing contact.</li>
                <li>Billing information — handled by Stripe on our behalf; we never see or store your full card number.</li>
                <li>Usage data — which features get used, API calls made through your integration keys, and error logs, so we can keep the service running and fix things that break.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>From the people your organization serves, if you use these features:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Queue tickets</em> &mdash; a name and phone number, if the organization asks for them at check-in, plus which counter served them and how long they waited.</li>
                <li><em>Visitor Management</em> &mdash; full name, phone, email, a national ID or passport number if the site requires it, a check-in photo if the organization's kiosk captures one, vehicle plate, host name, and purpose of visit. If a person is placed on a site's watchlist, the reason given for that flag is also stored — this is one of the more sensitive things we hold, and it exists purely so front-desk staff can be warned; we don't do anything with it beyond storing it and surfacing it back to that organization.</li>
                <li><em>Campaign Marketing</em> &mdash; contact details (email, phone, or a Telegram chat ID) that an organization uploads or collects, used to send messages the organization composes. Every message we send on their behalf includes a working unsubscribe link — that's not optional, it's built into the sending pipeline.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>3. How long we keep it</h2>
            <p>
                Visitor records are the one category with a built-in expiry: by default we hold them for 730
                days (two years) and then a nightly job permanently deletes them. Any organization can change
                that window to anywhere between 30 days and 10 years to match their own compliance needs — we
                don't second-guess that setting, it's theirs to configure. A visitor who's still checked in when
                their record would otherwise expire is never purged mid-visit; the clock only runs on completed
                visits.
            </p>
            <p>
                Queue ticket history and campaign send logs are kept for as long as the organization's account
                is active, since they're the basis for the reporting features we provide. If an organization
                closes its account, we keep a copy of its data for a short period in case of accidental
                cancellation, then delete it.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Who else sees it</h2>
            <p>We don't sell personal data, to anyone, ever. A small number of processors handle parts of the job on our behalf:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Stripe</strong> &mdash; payment processing for subscription billing.</li>
                <li><strong>SMS, email, WhatsApp, and Telegram gateways</strong> &mdash; only for organizations that actively use Campaign Marketing, and only to deliver the specific message that organization sent.</li>
                <li><strong>Our hosting and database provider</strong> &mdash; where everything above physically lives.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We hand data to law enforcement only when legally compelled to (a valid court order or
                equivalent), and we'll tell the affected organization unless we're legally barred from doing so.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Keeping it safe</h2>
            <p>
                Every connection to Q-Mgr runs over HTTPS. Passwords are hashed, never stored as plain text.
                Each organization's data is logically separated from every other organization's — a bug in one
                tenant's view can't leak another tenant's records, and this is enforced at the database query
                level, not just in the interface. Access to production data internally is limited to the people
                who need it to keep the service running. No system is unbreakable, and if something ever does go
                wrong badly enough to expose your data, we'll tell you promptly and plainly, not bury it in a
                blog post six months later.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Cookies and local storage</h2>
            <p>
                We use your browser's local storage to keep you signed in between visits and to remember small
                preferences like your last-selected branch. We don't run third-party advertising trackers, and
                we don't sell browsing data to ad networks — there isn't an ad network involved in Q-Mgr at all,
                on the admin side.
            </p>

            <h2>7. If you're a staff user of an organization on Q-Mgr</h2>
            <p>
                You can review and correct your own account details from your profile page at any time. To
                delete your account, ask your organization's admin, or email us at
                <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a> and we'll help.
            </p>

            <h2>8. If you're a customer or visitor of one of our clients</h2>
            <p>
                If an organization checked you into a queue, logged you as a visitor, or sent you a message
                through Q-Mgr, that organization decided to collect your data and is the right first place to
                ask about it — they can see, correct, or delete it directly from their admin dashboard. If you
                can't reach them, or you'd rather come to us first, email
                <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a> with the organization's name and
                what you'd like done, and we'll help route it and, where we're able to act directly, act on it.
            </p>

            <h2>9. Children</h2>
            <p>
                Q-Mgr's admin tools are built for organizations and their staff, not for children. We don't
                knowingly collect account data from anyone under 18. A visitor log entry for a minor (e.g. a
                patient at a clinic) is handled the same as any other visitor record, under the organization's
                own responsibility as the data controller.
            </p>

            <h2>10. Changes to this policy</h2>
            <p>
                If we make a material change to how we handle personal data, we'll update the effective date at
                the top of this page and, for changes that meaningfully affect existing customers, email the
                account admins directly rather than expecting anyone to notice a quiet edit.
            </p>

            <h2>11. Contact</h2>
            <p>
                Questions, requests, or concerns about any of the above: <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
                We read every email that comes in ourselves.
            </p>

